.TH MLTEST 1
.SH NAME
mltest \- test the inference device
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B mltest
[
.I model
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B Mltest
exercises
.IR ml (3)
end to end: the model crosses into the device as bytes, the host
compiles and runs it, and the answer comes back through
.IR math (2)'s
marshalling.
.PP
It runs against
.BR /lib/ml/linear.onnx ,
built by
.B mkonnxmodel.py
alongside the emu source with weights chosen by hand rather than
trained, so the answer is known in advance:
.I y
=
.IR Wx " + " b
with
.I W
= [[1,2,3],[10,20,30]] and
.I b
= [0.5,\-0.5], so
.I x
= (1,2,3) must give
.I y
= (14.5, 139.5).
.PP
Asserting that, rather than that some bytes came back, is the point.
The two outputs differ by an order of magnitude and neither is symmetric
in its inputs, so a transposed weight matrix, a swapped output, a wrong
element width or a byte-order mistake each produce a visibly wrong
number instead of a plausible one.
Sending the input in the host's byte order rather than the device's
gives (0.5, \-0.5) \(em the bias alone \(em which is how that path was
checked.
.PP
Three further things are checked because each is a way the device could
appear to work while being wrong.
The declared element type is compared against
.BR f64 ,
since a client assuming
.B f32
would write half the bytes.
A deliberately short write to
.B in
must be refused rather than padded.
And the model is loaded a second time with
.B "units cpu"
and
.BR info 's
.B device
line must then say
.BR cpu :
a device report that is really a constant string would satisfy every
other check here.
.PP
Last, an fd on an instance is kept open past the
.B ctl
fd that owned it \(em which
.IR ml (3)
allows on purpose \(em and must then be refused rather than resolving to
whichever instance reused the slot.
That one does not fail loudly when it is broken: the write reaches the
new instance and succeeds if it has a model loaded, so the check
compares the error returned and not merely that one was.
.PP
Given a
.I model
argument it uses that file instead, in which case only the checks that
hold for any model are meaningful.
.SS "The wider fixture"
.B /lib/ml/linear.onnx
is a single
.BR Gemm .
Agreeing with
.I onnxruntime
on one
.B Gemm
says nothing about broadcasting,
.BR Softmax ,
.B Transpose
or
.BR Concat ,
which is where a hand-written evaluator goes wrong: an axis off by one, a
stride taken from the wrong shape, a broadcast that is right only because both
operands happened to be the same size.
.PP
.B /lib/ml/mlp.onnx
reaches all of them in one graph, with two rows in so the batch dimension is
open and the broadcast has to stretch.
The two backends agree on it to 6e\-08, which is
.B f32
rounding.
Dropping the stretch rule from the broadcast moves that to 0.57.
.SS "The two backends are compared"
Proving that whichever backend ran gives the right answer says nothing about
the other one.
.I Mltest
loads the same fixture on each in turn and requires them to agree.
Not to the bit \(em the software backend computes in
.B f64
and
.I onnxruntime
in
.B f32
\(em but to about a part in ten thousand, which is far tighter than any
structural mistake.
Flipping a single transpose flag in the software backend's row-major to
column-major
.B gemm
mapping makes it report 21.5 and 41.5 against the host's 14.5 and 139.5, so the
comparison has been seen to fail when it should.
.PP
Where there is no host backend \(em native Inferno, or an
.I emu
built without one \(em there is nothing to compare against, and the software
path standing alone is the whole result.
.I Mltest
says which of the two happened rather than passing silently.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/mltest.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR ml (3),
.IR math (2)
.SH BUGS
One model, one input, one output, all multi-array and all
.BR f64 .
Nothing here exercises
.B feed
or
.BR take ,
which exist for models with more than one of either.
.PP
Where there is no host backend the only check on the wider fixture is that
each
.B Softmax
row sums to one, and that is much weaker than it looks.
Breaking the software backend's broadcasting deliberately \(em dropping the
rule that a dimension of 1 stretches \(em moves the result by 0.57 against the
host, and every row still sums to one, because
.B Softmax
normalises whatever it is given.
So on native Inferno this fixture proves the graph ran and says little about
whether it ran correctly.
The oracle is what has the power here, and native Inferno has no oracle.
.PP
Nothing is timed, so this says the device is correct and nothing at all
about whether reaching the Neural Engine through it is worth doing.
